#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Monitor panel for the xfce4 panel button.

Top to bottom, in the order things are needed: the mode (Auto / Movie / Game); one sentence
for what the screens are doing now; today's brightness curve with the four numbers that
shape it, each changed by dragging it; the one control that belongs to the current mode
(Game: Shadows, drawn as the tone curve it applies); then the desk itself, every screen
where it stands and each one its own brightness control (drag it up or down), an All
track, and the clicked screen's contrast, speaker and power.

Brightness is continuous: it glides between the four levels as the light changes. The
dashed line across a screen is where automatic brightness wants it; a screen set by hand
is pinned and says so.

Monitors from different makers do not look alike at the same number. Set them by eye until
they do and press "These look equally bright → remember": from then on automatic brightness
and the All track keep them looking alike.

Brightness and contrast go through the brightness service (monitor-automation) when it
runs, so there is exactly one writer per monitor; without it the panel talks to the
monitors itself with ddcutil (needs ddcutil and the i2c group; it says what is missing
otherwise). Monitors are matched to xrandr outputs through their EDID. The bus↔monitor
mapping and capabilities are cached in ~/.cache/monitor-panel so the panel opens instantly;
presets and monitor names live in ~/.config/monitor-panel/config.json.
Clicking the panel button again, Escape, or clicking elsewhere closes it.
"""
